Below is the full end-to-end trace from the chat pop-up through the gateway, backend, and on to the TicketDeflection Foundry application. It is written to show both the technical plumbing and the logical behavior at each step.

---

## 1. The chat pop-up and the front-end shell

### What the user sees
- The user opens the portal page.
- A chat widget or pop-up appears, typically rendered from a static page asset.
- In your current repo, that is app.js plus supporting HTML and config.js.

### What builds the chat pop-up
- The current POC is using Microsoft Bot Framework Web Chat.
- app.js loads `window.WebChat.renderWebChat(...)`.
- It uses the portal user identity from `window.POC_CONFIG.portalUser`.
- The page displays a status line and a chat container.

### What happens before the user types
- On load, app.js calls `startChat()`.
- `startChat()` first obtains a token from either:
  - direct `Direct Line /tokens/generate` if a Direct Line secret is configured, or
  - the token broker function at `https://func-ticketdeflect-token-c1-03311835.azurewebsites.net/api/directline/token`.

So the very first technical step is:

1. Browser loads static JS and HTML
2. JS builds the chat UI object
3. JS requests auth credentials needed to connect the chat channel

---

## 2. The user enters a query

### The user action
- The user types a question like: “How do I escalate a ticket?” and presses send.
- The chat widget captures the chat bubble and sends it through its configured channel.

### In the current Web Chat implementation
- Web Chat forwards that message to the Direct Line service.
- Direct Line keeps the conversation state and maps the browser user to a Direct Line user ID.
- That means the browser is no longer directly sending the user message to your portal backend: it is sending it to Bot Service infrastructure.

This is the old path. It is important because it is what caused the mismatch:

- portal auth was already valid
- but the browser was now talking through a different channel path
- that channel path is what injected the login/card behavior

---

## 3. The concrete new path we want

To trace the intended final flow, replace the Direct Line segment with:

```text
Browser chat UI
      |
      v
     APIM
      |
      v
 Azure Function BFF
      |
      v
Azure AI Foundry application
```

In this target design, the browser sends the user message directly to a managed API endpoint instead of Direct Line.

---

## 4. What APIM is and how it is set up

### APIM role
APIM is the gateway. It is the public endpoint that the browser calls.

Its responsibilities:
- provide one stable API URL
- enforce allowed origins and CORS
- rate-limit chat requests
- reject malformed or abusive traffic
- optionally validate that requests come from the portal
- log every request for analytics
- hide the real backend endpoint from the browser

### Typical APIM configuration
In APIM you define:
- an API named something like `ticketdeflection-chat`
- an operation like `POST /chat`
- a backend target pointing to the Azure Function URL

Inbound policy examples:
- `validate-jwt` or `check-header` if you want additional portal-level trust
- `cors` to allow only your portal domain
- `rate-limit` to prevent floods
- `set-header` to add correlation IDs or request metadata
- `rewrite-uri` to map the public route to the function route

Outbound policy examples:
- `log-to-application-insights`
- `set-header` to remove internal identifiers

So when the browser says:

```http
POST https://{apim}.azure-api.net/chat
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sessionId":"portal-123",
  "message":"How do I escalate a ticket?"
}
```

APIM first handles the traffic and only then forwards it to the backend.

---

## 5. Why we use Azure Function as the BFF

### What the Function is for
The Function is the back-end-for-frontend: it is the only component that sees both
- the portal-level request
- and the Foundry API contract

Its jobs are:
- validate the incoming chat request
- enforce schema and message size limits
- associate the browser session with a chat thread
- acquire secure credentials for Foundry
- call the Foundry app endpoint
- normalize the Foundry result
- return a clean response to the portal

### Why not just call Foundry from the browser?
Because the browser cannot safely hold:
- a Foundry API key
- a managed identity
- a secret
- or a long-lived token

So the Function is the safe holder of the Foundry credentials.

### Why not only Bot Service?
Bot Service adds a second auth/gateway layer and it is the layer that injected the login prompt. The new design intentionally removes that extra channel and replaces it with a direct API pattern.

### What the Function will do technically
A request arrives from APIM to the Function.

The Function code does something like:
- parse body: `{ sessionId, message, metadata }`
- if no `sessionId`, generate one
- optionally validate the portal user header or session cookie
- create a Foundry request payload
- call Foundry using a service token
- receive Foundry response
- filter it
- return it to APIM

---

## 6. How the request reaches Foundry

### What the Foundry endpoint looks like
A Foundry application endpoint typically looks like:

```text
POST https://AIFoundryLab-01.services.ai.azure.com/api/projects/AIFoundryLab-01-Project/applications/TicketDeflection-NoLogin/response?api-version=2026-01-15-preview
```

or it may be a project-level Responses endpoint with the application name inside the body.

### How the Function calls it
The Function obtains a Bearer token using managed identity:
- `DefaultAzureCredential` in Node/Python
- `ManagedIdentityCredential` if configured explicitly

Then it sends:

```http
POST https://.../response?api-version=...
Authorization: Bearer <token>
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "input": {
    "text": "How do I escalate a ticket?"
  },
  "conversation": {
    "id": "portal-123",
    "conversationType": "user"
  },
  "channel": "portal",
  "metadata": {
    "portalUserId": "poc-user-001",
    "source": "portal-chat"
  }
}
```

The exact body shape depends on the Responses API contract, but the main idea is:
- send the user's text
- send the conversation/thread id
- send any useful channel or metadata flags

### What the Function expects back
Foundry returns a structured response, for example:
- answer text
- cards or suggestions
- state information
- any follow-up or action metadata

The Function then strips anything unsafe and returns only what the portal UI needs.

